1
00:00:00,470 --> 00:00:03,873
You're hurrying down the steps.
There's ice on one of them.

2
00:00:03,940 --> 00:00:08,395
Your knee twists the wrong way,
and you hear it before you feel it.

3
00:00:08,462 --> 00:00:11,504
Twenty minutes later: a waiting room.
An X-ray.

4
00:00:11,571 --> 00:00:14,446
A painkiller. A doctor you've never met.

5
00:00:14,513 --> 00:00:17,766
By the evening,
someone is sitting by your bed.

6
00:00:17,833 --> 00:00:20,318
Six weeks, and you'll walk again.

7
00:00:20,385 --> 00:00:24,624
Every one of those things
was invented in the last 200 years.

8
00:00:24,691 --> 00:00:28,380
People have been
breaking their legs for 300,000.

9
00:00:28,447 --> 00:00:30,291
Now take all of it away.

10
00:00:30,434 --> 00:00:33,880
No hospital. No X-ray. No painkiller.

11
00:00:33,947 --> 00:00:36,497
Nobody who has ever heard of germs.

12
00:00:36,564 --> 00:00:39,123
31,000 years ago.

13
00:00:39,190 --> 00:00:41,995
If this happened to you here,
would you live?

14
00:00:42,573 --> 00:00:45,476
For a long time, the answer was no.

15
00:00:45,543 --> 00:00:47,828
Badly hurt meant finished.

16
00:00:47,895 --> 00:00:51,080
The group kept moving,
and the weak stayed behind.

17
00:00:51,147 --> 00:00:54,278
It's in films, in cartoons,
in school books.

18
00:00:54,345 --> 00:00:58,426
The bones tell a different story,
and it ends with you.

19
00:00:58,493 --> 00:01:00,413
So let's play a game.

20
00:01:00,480 --> 00:01:03,379
Six injuries. Six real people,

21
00:01:03,446 --> 00:01:08,323
from Spain to Iraq to Borneo,
whose skeletons still carry the marks.

22
00:01:08,390 --> 00:01:11,013
Same three questions every time.

23
00:01:11,080 --> 00:01:14,131
What does it do to your body?
What did they do about it?

24
00:01:14,198 --> 00:01:17,117
And would you live, and for how long?

25
00:01:17,249 --> 00:01:21,086
Make your guess before I tell you.
Keep score in the comments, if you like.

26
00:01:21,153 --> 00:01:23,157
Two things to keep in mind.

27
00:01:23,224 --> 00:01:25,719
One of these six did not make it.

28
00:01:25,786 --> 00:01:28,222
And one of them
survived an operation that,

29
00:01:28,289 --> 00:01:33,533
31,000 years later, still killed
one in four soldiers who had it.

30
00:01:33,600 --> 00:01:35,725
We'll save that one for last.

31
00:01:36,209 --> 00:01:38,775
Let's start with a pain you already know.

32
00:01:38,842 --> 00:01:42,846
You bite into something hard,
and a jolt shoots up through your jaw,

33
00:01:42,913 --> 00:01:45,324
into your ear, into your skull.

34
00:01:45,391 --> 00:01:48,479
Today you book a dentist,
and complain about the bill.

35
00:01:48,546 --> 00:01:50,816
Back then, you just kept chewing.

36
00:01:50,883 --> 00:01:52,676
And they chewed a lot.

37
00:01:52,743 --> 00:01:56,829
Gritty roots, hard seeds,
meat cooked over ash.

38
00:01:56,896 --> 00:01:59,606
Teeth cracked. Teeth wore down.

39
00:01:59,673 --> 00:02:04,052
Once the soft inside of a tooth is open,
bacteria move in.

40
00:02:04,119 --> 00:02:09,418
The tooth rots, pus builds up under it,
and the infection eats into the jaw.

41
00:02:09,485 --> 00:02:13,928
Left alone, it can reach the blood.
People still die from that today.

42
00:02:13,995 --> 00:02:17,134
In a cave in northern
Spain called El Sidrón,

43
00:02:17,201 --> 00:02:22,492
archaeologists found a Neanderthal
man who lived nearly 50,000 years ago.

44
00:02:22,559 --> 00:02:27,323
His jaw shows exactly that.
An abscess, eating into the bone.

45
00:02:27,390 --> 00:02:33,464
In 2017, a team led by the microbiologist
Laura Weyrich scraped the hard plaster off

46
00:02:33,531 --> 00:02:36,962
his teeth and read the DNA
trapped inside it.

47
00:02:37,029 --> 00:02:40,656
Plaque is a diary.
Every meal leaves a trace.

48
00:02:40,723 --> 00:02:44,111
They found mushrooms, pine nuts, moss.

49
00:02:44,221 --> 00:02:46,732
And two things nobody expected.

50
00:02:46,958 --> 00:02:48,875
The first was poplar.

51
00:02:48,942 --> 00:02:50,987
Nobody eats poplar for dinner.

52
00:02:51,054 --> 00:02:56,062
But its bark carries the same pain-killing
chemical that aspirin was later made from.

53
00:02:56,129 --> 00:02:59,850
The second was a mould.
Penicillium rubens.

54
00:02:59,917 --> 00:03:05,135
It's the same species of mould Alexander
Fleming found growing in his lab in 1928,

55
00:03:05,202 --> 00:03:07,397
the day he discovered penicillin.

56
00:03:07,706 --> 00:03:10,252
Did this man know what he was doing?

57
00:03:10,396 --> 00:03:11,547
We can't prove it.

58
00:03:11,614 --> 00:03:15,225
He might have swallowed the mould
on old plants, by accident.

59
00:03:15,292 --> 00:03:17,857
But here is a man with a throbbing jaw,

60
00:03:17,924 --> 00:03:24,105
chewing on a pain-killing bark, almost
50,000 years before aspirin came in a box.

61
00:03:24,326 --> 00:03:26,951
And he wasn't the only one at El Sidrón.

62
00:03:27,018 --> 00:03:29,548
In another Neanderthal from the same cave,

63
00:03:29,615 --> 00:03:34,068
a team led by Karen Hardy found
traces of yarrow and chamomile.

64
00:03:34,135 --> 00:03:38,130
Both taste bitter.
Both give you almost no food.

65
00:03:38,197 --> 00:03:42,935
And these Neanderthals carried the gene
that makes bitter taste strong.

66
00:03:43,002 --> 00:03:44,846
So why eat them?

67
00:03:44,980 --> 00:03:49,873
For thousands of years, people have put
yarrow on wounds and taken it for fevers.

68
00:03:49,940 --> 00:03:52,416
Chamomile, for upset stomachs.

69
00:03:52,490 --> 00:03:55,102
One person's plaque
can't prove a pharmacy.

70
00:03:55,169 --> 00:03:57,233
But it's hard to explain as dinner.

71
00:03:57,300 --> 00:03:59,358
And it gets more direct.

72
00:03:59,425 --> 00:04:03,043
In the Italian Alps,
about 14,000 years ago,

73
00:04:03,110 --> 00:04:06,871
a young man was buried in a rock
shelter called Villabruna.

74
00:04:06,938 --> 00:04:09,911
One of his back teeth had a big cavity.

75
00:04:09,978 --> 00:04:11,315
Under a microscope,

76
00:04:11,382 --> 00:04:15,133
the walls of that cavity
are covered in tiny scratches.

77
00:04:15,200 --> 00:04:17,590
Someone had scraped the rotten part out,

78
00:04:17,657 --> 00:04:20,631
with a pointed flint,
while he was still alive.

79
00:04:20,698 --> 00:04:23,946
It's the oldest dental
work anyone has found.

80
00:04:24,259 --> 00:04:26,316
No anaesthetic. No drill.

81
00:04:26,383 --> 00:04:29,911
A sharp stone,
and someone holding your head still.

82
00:04:30,038 --> 00:04:34,509
Next time you're in the dentist's chair,
remember: at least it isn't a rock.

83
00:04:34,576 --> 00:04:36,350
So, your guess?

84
00:04:36,486 --> 00:04:39,912
Would you live? Yes. In a lot of pain.

85
00:04:40,490 --> 00:04:42,760
Now the one I warned you about.

86
00:04:42,827 --> 00:04:47,911
High in the mountains of northern Iraq
there's a huge cave called Shanidar.

87
00:04:47,978 --> 00:04:49,385
In the 1950s,

88
00:04:49,452 --> 00:04:54,800
an archaeologist named Ralph Solecki found
the remains of several Neanderthals there.

89
00:04:54,867 --> 00:04:59,451
One of them, known as Shanidar 3,
was a man in his forties.

90
00:04:59,518 --> 00:05:02,625
On one of his left ribs, there's a groove.

91
00:05:02,951 --> 00:05:08,021
Something sharp went in between his ribs,
from the side, at a downward angle.

92
00:05:08,088 --> 00:05:13,172
In 2009, a team led by Steven
Churchill tried to work out what.

93
00:05:13,239 --> 00:05:16,541
They threw stone-tipped
weapons into pig carcasses,

94
00:05:16,608 --> 00:05:19,511
again and again, and compared the damage.

95
00:05:19,578 --> 00:05:23,532
The closest match was a light spear,
thrown from a distance.

96
00:05:23,599 --> 00:05:26,533
They can't rule out an accident,
or a knife.

97
00:05:26,600 --> 00:05:28,948
But a thrown weapon fits best.

98
00:05:29,015 --> 00:05:31,287
Here's the part that's hard to look at.

99
00:05:31,354 --> 00:05:34,397
The bone around the groove
had started to heal.

100
00:05:34,464 --> 00:05:36,940
So he didn't die on the spot.

101
00:05:37,166 --> 00:05:41,197
Someone got him back to the cave.
Someone kept him alive.

102
00:05:41,264 --> 00:05:42,908
Picture those weeks.

103
00:05:42,975 --> 00:05:46,804
Every breath hurts. Lying down hurts more.

104
00:05:46,871 --> 00:05:50,378
Someone brings water.
Someone keeps the fire going at night,

105
00:05:50,445 --> 00:05:53,349
so the cold doesn't finish
what the spear started.

106
00:05:53,416 --> 00:05:55,968
The wound closes on the outside.

107
00:05:56,035 --> 00:05:59,231
For a few weeks,
it looks like he might make it.

108
00:05:59,298 --> 00:06:00,496
And then he died.

109
00:06:00,563 --> 00:06:04,161
Possibly from the damage to his lung,
which nobody could reach.

110
00:06:04,643 --> 00:06:08,941
Some wounds were simply beyond anyone,
however much they cared.

111
00:06:09,008 --> 00:06:11,876
Would you live? No. A few weeks.

112
00:06:12,429 --> 00:06:15,256
How dangerous was their life, really?

113
00:06:15,394 --> 00:06:19,819
In 1995, two scientists
compared Neanderthal injuries

114
00:06:19,886 --> 00:06:22,450
with every modern group they could find.

115
00:06:22,517 --> 00:06:25,088
The closest match? Rodeo riders.

116
00:06:25,155 --> 00:06:27,570
People who get thrown
off bulls for a living.

117
00:06:27,637 --> 00:06:31,871
Years later, another team went
through modern hospital records again.

118
00:06:31,938 --> 00:06:35,900
And the closest match turned
out to be something less heroic.

119
00:06:35,967 --> 00:06:40,058
People falling off water tubes,
and walking into tables.

120
00:06:40,145 --> 00:06:43,323
Which, honestly, sounds more like us.

121
00:06:43,549 --> 00:06:46,453
Either way, they got hurt a lot.

122
00:06:46,520 --> 00:06:51,038
And back in that same cave in Iraq
lies the man who shows it best.

123
00:06:51,105 --> 00:06:53,473
Scientists call him Shanidar 1.

124
00:06:53,540 --> 00:06:57,564
If you've seen our film on the eight
human species, you've already met him.

125
00:06:57,631 --> 00:07:00,231
He lived around 50,000 years ago,

126
00:07:00,298 --> 00:07:03,624
and his skeleton reads
like a list of disasters.

127
00:07:03,691 --> 00:07:08,568
A blow to the left side of his face,
which probably left him blind in that eye.

128
00:07:08,635 --> 00:07:12,624
A right arm that withered away,
with the lower half gone.

129
00:07:12,691 --> 00:07:16,088
A damaged right leg,
which would have made him limp.

130
00:07:16,155 --> 00:07:22,009
And in 2017, Erik Trinkaus and Sébastien
Villotte found one more thing.

131
00:07:22,076 --> 00:07:25,375
Bony growths had almost
closed his ear canals.

132
00:07:25,442 --> 00:07:27,589
He was probably close to deaf.

133
00:07:27,656 --> 00:07:29,795
So picture yourself in his place.

134
00:07:29,862 --> 00:07:32,679
Half blind. One arm. A limp.

135
00:07:32,746 --> 00:07:37,042
Barely able to hear a big cat behind him,
or a warning shout.

136
00:07:37,109 --> 00:07:42,240
In a land full of predators, a man like
that should be dead within a season.

137
00:07:42,307 --> 00:07:43,379
He wasn't.

138
00:07:43,902 --> 00:07:46,432
He lived for years after those injuries,

139
00:07:46,499 --> 00:07:51,481
to an age somewhere between 35 and 50,
which was old for a Neanderthal.

140
00:07:51,548 --> 00:07:54,560
He couldn't have hunted much.
He couldn't have run.

141
00:07:54,627 --> 00:07:57,952
So every day,
someone shared their food with him.

142
00:07:58,019 --> 00:08:01,621
Every move to a new camp,
someone waited for him.

143
00:08:01,688 --> 00:08:04,547
You might have heard
about this cave's other famous grave,

144
00:08:04,614 --> 00:08:07,974
a Neanderthal
laid to rest on a bed of flowers.

145
00:08:08,041 --> 00:08:10,246
That part is probably not true.

146
00:08:10,313 --> 00:08:16,096
In 2023, a team showed the pollen was most
likely carried in by burrowing bees.

147
00:08:16,163 --> 00:08:20,328
But the man with one arm is real.
His bones are the proof.

148
00:08:20,395 --> 00:08:24,323
So, would you live?
Make your guess, then look at the board.

149
00:08:24,390 --> 00:08:28,595
Alone: No. With them: YES, for years.

150
00:08:29,077 --> 00:08:32,401
So far, these were accidents and attacks.

151
00:08:32,468 --> 00:08:35,646
But what if the problem
starts on the day you're born?

152
00:08:35,987 --> 00:08:38,938
In 1989, in a cave near Valencia,

153
00:08:39,005 --> 00:08:43,140
in Spain, archaeologists picked
up a small piece of skull.

154
00:08:43,207 --> 00:08:46,528
It sat in a drawer
for more than thirty years.

155
00:08:46,595 --> 00:08:51,448
In 2024, a team led by Mercedes
Conde-Valverde scanned it.

156
00:08:51,515 --> 00:08:55,221
It came from a Neanderthal child.
They called her Tina.

157
00:08:55,288 --> 00:08:58,076
Deep inside that bone sits the inner ear,

158
00:08:58,143 --> 00:09:02,032
a tiny spiral that controls
both hearing and balance.

159
00:09:02,099 --> 00:09:03,997
Tina's was shaped in a way that,

160
00:09:04,064 --> 00:09:07,172
in people,
is only seen with Down syndrome.

161
00:09:07,403 --> 00:09:10,660
For her, that meant severe hearing loss.

162
00:09:10,727 --> 00:09:16,267
And vertigo so strong that the ground
would tilt every time she tried to stand.

163
00:09:16,334 --> 00:09:19,334
Imagine trying to learn
to walk on a boat in a storm.

164
00:09:19,401 --> 00:09:20,482
Every day.

165
00:09:20,549 --> 00:09:22,763
Now imagine being her mother.

166
00:09:22,830 --> 00:09:27,983
The group moves camp every few weeks,
on foot, across hills and rivers.

167
00:09:28,050 --> 00:09:29,317
There's food to find,

168
00:09:29,384 --> 00:09:34,043
a fire to keep, and a child who
falls every time she lets go of her.

169
00:09:34,110 --> 00:09:37,011
She could not have kept
up with a group on the move.

170
00:09:37,078 --> 00:09:39,635
She could not have walked far on her own.

171
00:09:39,702 --> 00:09:43,090
And yet she lived to at least
six years old.

172
00:09:43,493 --> 00:09:45,111
One mother, alone,

173
00:09:45,178 --> 00:09:49,543
could not have managed that, while
also finding food and keeping a fire.

174
00:09:49,610 --> 00:09:52,401
Someone else carried her.
More than one person.

175
00:09:52,468 --> 00:09:53,686
For years.

176
00:09:53,753 --> 00:09:56,936
And remember,
this isn't even our own species.

177
00:09:57,003 --> 00:09:59,006
Neanderthals were doing this somewhere

178
00:09:59,073 --> 00:10:04,397
between 146,000 and 273,000 years ago.

179
00:10:04,464 --> 00:10:07,418
Would you live? Yes. To at least six.

180
00:10:07,485 --> 00:10:09,329
Because they didn't let go.

181
00:10:09,927 --> 00:10:13,464
Next, an injury that someone
gave you on purpose.

182
00:10:13,531 --> 00:10:19,390
In a museum in Lausanne, in Switzerland,
there's a skull about 5,500 years old.

183
00:10:19,457 --> 00:10:21,525
It has a round hole in it.

184
00:10:21,592 --> 00:10:26,022
The edges are clean, cut and scraped
through the bone with a sharp stone.

185
00:10:26,089 --> 00:10:29,532
A fight leaves cracks.
This was done carefully.

186
00:10:29,599 --> 00:10:33,188
It's called trepanation.
Surgery on the skull.

187
00:10:33,255 --> 00:10:36,034
We don't know why they did it.
Maybe headaches.

188
00:10:36,101 --> 00:10:39,586
Maybe fits.
Maybe something only they understood.

189
00:10:39,653 --> 00:10:41,652
But think about what it took.

190
00:10:41,719 --> 00:10:45,538
Someone had to hold the patient down,
or keep them calm.

191
00:10:45,605 --> 00:10:50,299
Someone scraped through the scalp and then
through the bone, a little at a time,

192
00:10:50,366 --> 00:10:53,428
careful not to go one millimetre too deep.

193
00:10:53,495 --> 00:10:57,601
The brain sits right under that bone.
One slip, and it's over.

194
00:10:57,668 --> 00:10:59,867
Now look at the edge of that hole.

195
00:10:59,934 --> 00:11:03,313
It's smooth and rounded. That's new bone.

196
00:11:03,380 --> 00:11:06,557
And bone only grows
back on a living person.

197
00:11:06,934 --> 00:11:11,601
So this patient survived the operation,
and lived long enough to heal.

198
00:11:11,668 --> 00:11:13,799
And they weren't a lucky one-off.

199
00:11:13,866 --> 00:11:16,521
In Ensisheim, in eastern France, a man

200
00:11:16,588 --> 00:11:21,949
of around fifty was buried about 7,000
years ago with two of these holes.

201
00:11:22,016 --> 00:11:24,989
One was about the size
of the palm of your hand.

202
00:11:25,056 --> 00:11:27,818
Around both of them,
the bone had grown back.

203
00:11:27,885 --> 00:11:30,369
The smaller one had closed completely.

204
00:11:30,436 --> 00:11:32,551
Two operations on his skull.

205
00:11:32,618 --> 00:11:38,040
With stone tools, nothing for the pain,
and no idea what an infection was.

206
00:11:38,107 --> 00:11:39,390
Your guess?

207
00:11:39,526 --> 00:11:43,468
Would you live? Yes. Twice. For years.

208
00:11:43,950 --> 00:11:47,449
This channel tells the story of us,
one film at a time.

209
00:11:47,516 --> 00:11:50,904
If you want the next one,
subscribe, and it'll find you.

210
00:11:51,451 --> 00:11:54,590
Which brings us to the last one.
The one I promised.

211
00:11:54,657 --> 00:11:58,538
Borneo. The east of the island.
Rainforest, and limestone

212
00:11:58,605 --> 00:12:04,104
mountains so steep that some caves can
only be reached by boat, or by climbing.

213
00:12:04,171 --> 00:12:08,561
In 2020, a team of Australian
and Indonesian archaeologists,

214
00:12:08,628 --> 00:12:12,765
led by Tim Maloney,
was digging in a cave called Liang Tebo.

215
00:12:12,832 --> 00:12:14,449
In these same mountains,

216
00:12:14,516 --> 00:12:19,069
people were painting animals on cave
walls at least 40,000 years ago.

217
00:12:19,136 --> 00:12:21,625
Some of the oldest paintings on Earth.

218
00:12:21,692 --> 00:12:26,425
So these were people with skill,
and patience, and time to make art.

219
00:12:26,492 --> 00:12:29,752
In Liang Tebo, the team found a grave.

220
00:12:29,819 --> 00:12:31,858
Inside it, a young person,

221
00:12:31,925 --> 00:12:38,009
about nineteen or twenty years old,
buried with care around 31,000 years ago.

222
00:12:38,076 --> 00:12:40,682
The skeleton was almost complete.

223
00:12:40,749 --> 00:12:42,383
Except for one thing.

224
00:12:42,862 --> 00:12:46,771
The left foot was missing.
So was the bottom of the left leg.

225
00:12:46,838 --> 00:12:51,751
Bones go missing from graves all the time.
Animals, water, time.

226
00:12:51,818 --> 00:12:53,597
But this was different.

227
00:12:53,664 --> 00:12:56,586
A broken bone ends in jagged splinters.

228
00:12:56,653 --> 00:12:59,065
A chewed one carries tooth marks.

229
00:12:59,132 --> 00:13:02,601
This one ends in a single clean,
slanted cut,

230
00:13:02,668 --> 00:13:04,933
through both bones of the lower leg.

231
00:13:05,254 --> 00:13:07,604
And the bone had sealed over it.

232
00:13:07,671 --> 00:13:11,348
The team could read that healing
almost like tree rings.

233
00:13:11,415 --> 00:13:14,879
The leg was removed
when this person was still a child.

234
00:13:14,946 --> 00:13:17,774
And they lived another six to nine years.

235
00:13:17,841 --> 00:13:21,465
We don't know what happened to that leg.
A fall in the rocks, maybe.

236
00:13:21,532 --> 00:13:24,227
The team suggests
something like a rockfall.

237
00:13:24,294 --> 00:13:28,112
But picture the moment the adults
around that child decided.

238
00:13:28,179 --> 00:13:32,198
The foot can't be saved.
If it stays, the child dies.

239
00:13:32,265 --> 00:13:35,049
If we cut, maybe the child lives.

240
00:13:35,116 --> 00:13:36,539
And they cut.

241
00:13:37,188 --> 00:13:39,185
Think about what that takes.

242
00:13:39,252 --> 00:13:43,627
Someone knew where to cut,
and how, so the bone would heal.

243
00:13:43,694 --> 00:13:45,769
Someone stopped the bleeding.

244
00:13:45,836 --> 00:13:47,523
In a tropical rainforest,

245
00:13:47,590 --> 00:13:52,483
where an open wound can turn bad in days,
there is no sign of infection.

246
00:13:52,550 --> 00:13:56,433
The scientists think they must
have known which plants to use.

247
00:13:56,500 --> 00:13:59,940
And then,
someone looked after a child with one leg,

248
00:14:00,007 --> 00:14:02,845
in mountains you can
barely climb with two.

249
00:14:02,912 --> 00:14:04,405
For years.

250
00:14:04,890 --> 00:14:07,169
Now, the promise from the start.

251
00:14:07,236 --> 00:14:11,080
In the 1860s,
during the American Civil War, surgeons

252
00:14:11,147 --> 00:14:16,921
with steel saws and chloroform amputated
tens of thousands of arms and legs.

253
00:14:16,988 --> 00:14:20,166
About one in four of those soldiers died.

254
00:14:20,664 --> 00:14:22,913
31,000 years earlier,

255
00:14:22,980 --> 00:14:28,238
with stone tools, a child in Borneo
went through the same thing, and lived.

256
00:14:28,305 --> 00:14:34,155
Before this, the oldest amputation anyone
had found was about 7,000 years old.

257
00:14:34,222 --> 00:14:38,453
This one pushed it back by 24,000 years.

258
00:14:38,763 --> 00:14:42,385
Would you live? Yes.
Six to nine more years.

259
00:14:42,452 --> 00:14:44,085
Somebody made sure.

260
00:14:44,908 --> 00:14:46,903
Look at the board.

261
00:14:46,970 --> 00:14:50,217
A toothache. A spear. A crushed body.

262
00:14:50,284 --> 00:14:53,478
A child born different.
Two holes in a skull.

263
00:14:53,545 --> 00:14:54,982
A leg cut off.

264
00:14:55,049 --> 00:14:58,297
Five times out of six, the answer was yes.

265
00:14:58,680 --> 00:15:03,062
Even the man with the spear wound got
weeks he would never have had alone.

266
00:15:03,129 --> 00:15:06,035
And not one of them could
have done it on their own.

267
00:15:06,102 --> 00:15:09,497
The man at Shanidar needed
someone to share food.

268
00:15:09,564 --> 00:15:12,047
Tina needed someone to carry her.

269
00:15:12,114 --> 00:15:17,762
The child in Borneo needed someone to cut,
someone to clean, and someone to wait.

270
00:15:17,829 --> 00:15:22,531
Back then, the most dangerous thing that
could happen to you was being left alone.

271
00:15:23,012 --> 00:15:26,401
And the old story,
that they left the weak behind?

272
00:15:26,534 --> 00:15:29,501
The ground is full of people
who were carried.

273
00:15:30,162 --> 00:15:33,287
Today, when you get hurt,
you call a number.

274
00:15:33,354 --> 00:15:35,058
They called a name.

275
00:15:35,284 --> 00:15:40,081
You get a stranger in a uniform,
and a machine that sees through your skin.

276
00:15:40,148 --> 00:15:43,115
They got the people who
sat with them by the fire.

277
00:15:43,346 --> 00:15:45,467
You get painkillers in a bottle.

278
00:15:45,534 --> 00:15:49,694
They got bark, and mould,
and someone who knew which tree.

279
00:15:49,915 --> 00:15:54,421
You get six weeks off,
and a hundred messages asking how you are.

280
00:15:54,488 --> 00:15:57,947
They got someone who slowed down,
so they could keep up.

281
00:15:58,509 --> 00:16:03,319
So the next time you're hurt, and someone
sits by your bed and holds your hand,

282
00:16:03,386 --> 00:16:05,230
look at that hand.

283
00:16:05,633 --> 00:16:08,108
That's the oldest medicine we have.

